mats-qemu: QEMU for the Mats virtual target

The archives this repository publishes hold qemu-system-arm and the shared
libraries it loads, built from upstream source releases with the patches
in patches/ (one, to GLib's configure on macOS), which ship in the sources
bundle too. Each component keeps its own licence:

  QEMU           GPL-2.0-only (some files under compatible licences; see
                 its LICENSE)
  dtc (libfdt)   GPL-2.0-or-later OR BSD-2-Clause, built into QEMU,
                 which is GPL-2.0 and carries it as such; its source is
                 in the sources bundle
  GLib           LGPL-2.1-or-later
  PCRE2          BSD-3-Clause WITH PCRE2-exception
  libffi         MIT
  zlib           Zlib
  proxy-libintl  LGPL-2.0-or-later (the macOS and Windows archives only)
  winpthreads    MIT AND BSD-3-Clause-Clear (the Windows archive only:
                 MSYS2's build of mingw-w64's POSIX threads library,
                 libwinpthread-1.dll, from the packages pins.json pins;
                 their exact source package is in the sources bundle)
  libgcc         GPL-3.0-or-later WITH GCC-exception-3.1 (the Windows
                 archive only: GCC's runtime library, linked into each
                 file with -static-libgcc. The exception permits that
                 whatever the licence of the program it is linked into,
                 provided the program comes from an Eligible Compilation
                 Process; this archive does, being built by GCC with no
                 plugins. Its texts are in licenses/libgcc/)

Each licence's text is in the archive's licenses/<component>/ folder.

Source: every release qemu-<version>-<n> of
https://github.com/haoliu0419/mats-qemu carries
mats-qemu-<version>-<n>-sources.tar.xz, which holds the source of each
component pins.json names exactly as it was fetched: the upstream tarballs
of QEMU, dtc, GLib, PCRE2, libffi, zlib and proxy-libintl, and MSYS2's
source package of the winpthreads build the Windows archive carries; every
patch; and the scripts and workflow that built that release's archives.
libgcc comes with the Windows toolchain and is not in it: the GCC Runtime
Library Exception does not require its source to be offered. The bundle
stays published for as long as the release does. This repository's
scripts are under the GPL-2.0 (LICENSE).

GLib, PCRE2, libffi, zlib, proxy-libintl and winpthreads are linked
dynamically: each is a separate file in the archive that can be replaced by
a build of the same library from its source.
